<%# The room itself: wallpaper, the top edge and the two side ornaments.

    Expects: theme, pieces (array of "photo" | "cornice" | "columns"), fixed (boolean).

    This partial owns the LAYERS - what is drawn, in what order, pinned or not. What each layer
    looks like belongs to the skin (views/partials/skins/<name>/), because a room made of gilded
    stone and a room made of roses share their structure and nothing else.

    Drawn as separate pieces rather than as one wide picture because a single SVG scaled to cover
    crops its own edges away at wide aspect ratios - and the edges are exactly where the side
    ornaments are. How each piece copes with being fitted to its box is the skin's problem and is
    explained in its own file; the gilded hall stretches, the rose bower crops.

    `fixed` is what makes the room page-wide: the layer is pinned to the viewport, so the page
    scrolls INSIDE it and the ornaments keep their tops and bottoms on screen instead of being one
    enormous shaft the reader only ever sees the middle of. %>
<div class="throne-hall<%= fixed ? ' throne-hall--fixed' : '' %>" aria-hidden="true">
  <% if (pieces.includes("photo") && theme.backdrop.mode === "photo") { %>
    <%# The photograph sits UNDER the drawn room, never instead of it: the top edge and the side
        ornaments are what keep the composition readable while a large backdrop is downloading. %>
    <img class="throne-hall__photo"
         src="<%= theme.backdrop.uploadUrl || ('/img/themes/' + theme.backdrop.preset + '.webp') %>"
         alt="" loading="eager" decoding="async" />
  <% } %>

  <% if (pieces.includes("cornice")) { %>
    <%- include('skins/' + theme.theme + '/cornice') %>
  <% } %>

  <% if (pieces.includes("columns")) { %>
    <% ["left", "right"].forEach(function(side) { %>
      <%- include('skins/' + theme.theme + '/columns', { side }) %>
    <% }) %>
  <% } %>
</div>
